tel 1.0

base espionage
import soundness

info   a client for the Agent Client Protocol, version 1: drive a coding agent through sessions, prompts and tool calls
detail
    _Espionage_ is the client half of the [Agent Client
    Protocol](https://agentclientprotocol.com/), version 1: the role an editor plays towards a
    coding agent such as Claude Code or Gemini CLI, here for programmatic use. A program
    launches an agent as a subprocess (or reaches one behind a pair of streams), opens a
    session, sends prompts, and watches the agent's work stream back — message chunks,
    thoughts, tool calls, plans — while answering the requests the agent makes of it:
    permission to act, file reads and writes, terminals.

    The protocol is JSON-RPC in both directions at once, and a prompt is not a function call
    but a **turn**: one long-lived request whose response arrives only after everything the
    agent did inside it. The library models the turn directly. `Acp.connect` takes two blocks:
    the first registers handlers on a lent `Acp.Registry` with the combinators `Acp.updated`,
    `Acp.permission`, `Acp.readFile`, `Acp.writeFile` and `Acp.terminals`; the second holds the
    live `Acp.Connection`, whose `prompt` blocks until the agent reports a `StopReason` while
    the turn's updates stream concurrently to the registered handler. The capabilities the
    client advertises at `initialize` are derived from what was registered, so the declaration
    can never disagree with the implementation.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
    import probates.awaitProbate
    import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import threads.virtualThreads
    import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

    supervise:
      Acp.connect(Acp.Agent(sh"claude-code-acp")):
        Acp.updated:
          Acp.update match
            case Acp.AgentMessageChunk(Acp.TextContent(text, _)) => Out.print(text)
            case other                                           => ()

        Acp.permission:
          Acp.options.filter(_.kind == Acp.PermissionOptionKind.AllowOnce).head
          . lay(Acp.Cancelled): option => Acp.Selected(option.optionId)

      . apply:
          val session = Acp.connection.newSession(t"/workspace")
          Acp.connection.prompt(session.sessionId, t"Fix the failing test")
    ```

    A connection needs, as contextual values, a `Monitor` (it runs a reader task and a writer
    task, so it sits inside `supervise`), a `Probate`, `Diagnostics`, a `WorkingDirectory` and
    an `Environment` for launching the agent, and a `Tactic[Acp.Error]`. Every handler is lent
    the session id it concerns and its payloads, read with the contextual accessors
    `Acp.sessionId`, `Acp.update`, `Acp.toolCall`, `Acp.options`, `Acp.path`, `Acp.line`,
    `Acp.limit` and `Acp.content`, and an `Emit[Acp.Error]`, so a fault raised in a handler
    becomes an error response to the agent rather than a hung turn.

    Every message type is a case class or enum nested in `Acp`, each with its JSON codec in
    its companion: `SessionUpdate` and its eight cases, `ToolCall` and `ToolCallUpdate`,
    `PermissionOption`, `StopReason`, the capability records, and the content blocks, which
    ACP adopts from the Model Context Protocol and which are re-exported here from
    `synesthesia.Content`, so content crosses between the two protocols without translation.

    Several of `Acp`'s members — `update`, `path`, `content`, `options`, `line`, `connection`,
    `Cancelled` — are names other libraries use too, which is why the umbrella exports `Acp`
    and not its contents. Write them as `Acp.update`, or `import Acp.*` inside the object which
    uses them, as the demo does.

    The stdio framing is newline-delimited JSON, one compact UTF-8 message per line, split on
    the raw linefeed byte before any decoding. A message is delivered the moment its newline
    arrives: the framer peeks at the terminator and advances past it without asking for more
    input, so on a live pipe it never waits for the agent's next message to deliver this one.
    Blank lines, and the remnant of a `\r\n` terminator, are skipped.

    The library has two source sets:

    - `espionage.core` — `Acp` and the `@rpc` wire traits (`AcpAgent`, `AcpClient` and their
      parts). It is built for the JVM only, since it depends on obligatory's JSON-RPC
      support, and needs capture checking.
    - `espionage.demo` — `DemoAcpClient`, a complete command-line client, not published as a
      library.

    `Acp` and the wire traits are exported to the `soundness` umbrella; the demo is not. The
    library takes no choice-package givens of its own, but a connection needs the named
    imports above (`threads`, `probates`, `workingDirectories`, `environments`, and an error
    strategy), because `import soundness.*` imports no givens.

refer espionage.Acp
refer espionage.Acp.connect
refer espionage.Acp.Connection
refer espionage.Acp.SessionUpdate
refer espionage.Acp.Error
refer synesthesia.Content

entry .Acp   the Agent Client Protocol: its message types, the handler registry, `connect`, and the connection
  detail
      `Acp` holds the whole client. Its members fall into four groups:

      - the **protocol types**, one case class or enum per ACP structure, each with its JSON
        codec in its companion: the capability records (`ClientCapabilities`,
        `AgentCapabilities`, …), sessions (`McpServer`, `SessionMode`, `NewSessionResult`),
        turns (`StopReason`, `PromptResult`), updates (`SessionUpdate` and its cases), tool
        calls (`ToolCall`, `ToolCallUpdate`, `ToolCallContent`), plans, permissions and the
        filesystem and terminal results, together with the content blocks re-exported from
        `synesthesia.Content`;
      - the **handler side**: the handler types (`UpdateHandler`, `PermissionHandler`,
        `ReadFileHandler`, `WriteFileHandler`, and the `Terminals` bundle), the contextual
        accessors a handler reads (`sessionId`, `update`, `toolCall`, `options`, `path`,
        `line`, `limit`, `content`), the registration combinators (`updated`, `permission`,
        `readFile`, `writeFile`, `terminals`, `capabilities`) and the `Registry` they write to;
      - the **connection side**: `Agent`, which says where the agent is, `connect`, which
        opens the exchange, `Connection`, the handle it lends, reached as `connection`, and
        `Observer`, which sees the raw traffic;
      - `Error`, which the connection raises and a handler emits.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      object Client:
        import Acp.*

        def register(using Acp.Registry^): Unit =
          updated:
            update match
              case AgentMessageChunk(TextContent(text, _)) => Out.print(text)
              case call: ToolCall                          => Out.println(call.title)
              case _                                       => ()

          readFile:
            t"contents of $path"
      ```

      Registration is possible wherever an `Acp.Registry^` is in scope — the first block of
      `connect`, or a helper such as `register` above which takes one — so a client can split
      its handlers across methods.
  refer espionage.Acp.connect
  refer espionage.Acp.Connection
  refer espionage.Acp.Registry
  refer espionage.Acp.SessionUpdate
  entry .version   the protocol version this library implements: `1`
      ACP versions are a single integer, incremented only for breaking changes. The client
      sends this at `initialize`, and `Connection#initialize` aborts with
      `Acp.Error.Reason.Incompatible` if the agent answers with any other.

  entry .Icon   an icon's source, MIME type, sizes and theme; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.Icon` is `synesthesia.Content.Icon`, shared with MCP, whose page has the full account.
  entry .Annotations   audience, priority and modification hints on content; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.Annotations` is `synesthesia.Content.Annotations`.
  entry .Role   who a piece of content is for, `User` or `Assistant`; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.Role` is `synesthesia.Content.Role`.
  entry .ContentBlock   a block of content: text, image, audio, resource link or embedded resource; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.ContentBlock` is `synesthesia.Content.ContentBlock`, whose page has the full
      account. A prompt is a `List[ContentBlock]`, and every message and thought chunk an agent
      streams carries one. It is discriminated on the wire by its `type` member.
  entry .TextContent   a block of text, with optional annotations; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.TextContent` is `synesthesia.Content.TextContent`. The usual pattern for streamed
      text is `case Acp.AgentMessageChunk(Acp.TextContent(text, _))`, and
      `Connection#prompt(sessionId, text)` wraps its text in one.
  entry .ImageContent   a Base64 image with its MIME type, and an optional URI; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.ImageContent` is `synesthesia.Content.ImageContent`. Its `uri` member is ACP's
      addition to MCP's shape, and vanishes from the wire when unset. An agent accepts images
      in a prompt only if its `PromptCapabilities#image` says so.
  entry .AudioContent   Base64 audio with its MIME type; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.AudioContent` is `synesthesia.Content.AudioContent`. An agent accepts audio in a
      prompt only if its `PromptCapabilities#audio` says so.
  entry .ResourceLink   a link to a resource by URI, with its name and optional metadata; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.ResourceLink` is `synesthesia.Content.ResourceLink`.
  entry .EmbeddedResource   a resource's contents carried inline; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.EmbeddedResource` is `synesthesia.Content.EmbeddedResource`. An agent accepts
      embedded context in a prompt only if its `PromptCapabilities#embeddedContext` says so.
  entry .Contents   a resource's contents, as text or as a blob; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.Contents` is `synesthesia.Content.Contents`.
  entry .ResourceContents   the common shape of a resource's contents: URI, MIME type and metadata; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.ResourceContents` is `synesthesia.Content.ResourceContents`.
  entry .TextResourceContents   a resource's contents as text; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.TextResourceContents` is `synesthesia.Content.TextResourceContents`.
  entry .BlobResourceContents   a resource's contents as a Base64 blob; re-exported from `synesthesia.Content`
      `Acp.BlobResourceContents` is `synesthesia.Content.BlobResourceContents`.

  entry .Envelope   the two members of any JSON-RPC message which decide how it is handled
    detail
        A message read only for its `method`, which a response lacks, and its `id`, which a
        notification lacks. Both are optional, so every message decodes: no method means a
        response, and no id a notification. The exchange's reader uses it to route each
        message without knowing its shape.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed envelope throws `Json.Error`
    entry #method   the method the message names, or `Unset` for a response
    entry #id   the id the message correlates on, as raw JSON, or `Unset` for a notification

  entry .FsCapabilities   whether the client serves the agent's file reads and writes
    detail
        Part of `ClientCapabilities`. `Acp.connect` sets `#readTextFile` exactly when a
        `readFile` handler was registered and `#writeTextFile` exactly when a `writeFile`
        handler was, so a conformant agent never asks for an operation the client cannot do.
    entry #readTextFile   `true` if the client answers `fs/read_text_file`
    entry #writeTextFile   `true` if the client answers `fs/write_text_file`

  entry .ClientCapabilities   what the client tells the agent it can do, at `initialize`
    detail
        Derived by `Acp.connect` from the registrations: `#fs` is present if either
        filesystem handler was registered, and `#terminal` is `true` if a `Terminals` bundle
        was. The `capabilities` combinator adjusts the derived value, last, for a client which
        must declare something the registrations do not imply.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        def register(using Acp.Registry^): Unit =
          Acp.readFile(t"")
          Acp.capabilities(_.copy(terminal = Unset))
        ```
    entry #fs   the filesystem capabilities, or `Unset` if the client serves no file operations
    entry #terminal   `true` if the client offers terminals, or `Unset`

  entry .PromptCapabilities   which kinds of content an agent accepts in a prompt
    detail
        Reported by the agent in `AgentCapabilities#promptCapabilities`. Text and resource
        links are always accepted; images, audio and embedded resources only where the
        agent says so.
    entry #image   whether `ImageContent` may appear in a prompt
    entry #audio   whether `AudioContent` may appear in a prompt
    entry #embeddedContext   whether `EmbeddedResource` content may appear in a prompt

  entry .McpCapabilities   which MCP transports, beyond stdio, the agent can connect to
    entry #http   whether the agent connects to MCP servers over HTTP
    entry #sse   whether the agent connects to MCP servers over server-sent events

  entry .AgentCapabilities   what the agent tells the client it can do, at `initialize`
    detail
        The agent's half of the capability negotiation, kept by the connection after the
        handshake and read as `Connection#agentCapabilities`. A client checks `#loadSession`
        before calling `Connection#loadSession`, and `#promptCapabilities` before putting
        anything but text and resource links in a prompt.
    entry #loadSession   whether the agent can resume a session with `session/load`
    entry #promptCapabilities   which content kinds the agent accepts in a prompt
    entry #mcpCapabilities   which MCP transports the agent can connect to

  entry .AuthMethod   one way the agent offers to authenticate the client
    detail
        Listed in `InitializeResult#authMethods`, and so in `Connection#authMethods`. When a
        request fails with `Acp.Error.Reason.AuthRequired`, the client answers with
        `Connection#authenticate(method.id)`.
    entry #id   the identifier to pass to `Connection#authenticate`
    entry #name   the method's display name
    entry #description   a longer description, if the agent gives one

  entry .InitializeResult   the agent's answer to `initialize`: its protocol version, capabilities and auth methods
    detail
        What `Connection#initialize` returns. `Acp.connect` performs the handshake itself, so a
        client reads the agent's answer as `Connection#agentCapabilities` and
        `Connection#authMethods` rather than handling this directly.
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed result throws `Json.Error`
    entry #protocolVersion   the version the agent speaks, which must be `Acp.version`
    entry #agentCapabilities   what the agent can do, if it says
    entry #authMethods   the ways the agent offers to authenticate, if any
    entry #_meta   the agent's implementation-defined metadata, as raw JSON

  entry .EnvVariable   an environment variable, by name and value
    detail
        Carried by `McpServer#env` for an MCP server the agent launches, and by a terminal the
        agent asks the client to create (`Terminals#create`).
    entry #name   the variable's name
    entry #value   the variable's value

  entry .McpServer   an MCP server the client wants the agent to connect to, by its launch command
    detail
        Passed to `Connection#newSession` or `Connection#loadSession`: the agent launches each
        server with the stdio transport and makes its tools available within the session.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        val server = Acp.McpServer(t"files", t"mcp-files", List(t"--root", t"/workspace"))
        Acp.connection.newSession(t"/workspace", List(server))
        ```
    entry #name   the server's name, as the agent will refer to it
    entry #command   the command which launches the server
    entry #args   the command's arguments
    entry #env   environment variables to set for the server

  entry .SessionMode   one mode an agent can work in, such as asking before every edit
    entry #id   the identifier to pass to `Connection#setMode`
    entry #name   the mode's display name
    entry #description   a longer description, if the agent gives one

  entry .SessionModeState   the modes a session offers, and which is current
    detail
        Reported in `NewSessionResult#modes`. The current mode changes with
        `Connection#setMode`, or at the agent's own initiative, reported as a
        `CurrentModeUpdate`.
    entry #currentModeId   the id of the mode the session is in
    entry #availableModes   every mode the session may switch to

  entry .NewSessionResult   the agent's answer to `session/new`: the session's id, and its modes
    detail
        Returned by `Connection#newSession`. `#sessionId` is what every later call about the
        session — `prompt`, `cancel`, `setMode`, `loadSession` — takes, and what every handler
        is lent as `Acp.sessionId`.
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed result throws `Json.Error`
    entry #sessionId   the new session's identifier
    entry #modes   the session's modes, if the agent offers any
    entry #_meta   the agent's implementation-defined metadata, as raw JSON

  entry .StopReason   why a prompt turn ended: `EndTurn`, `MaxTokens`, `MaxTurnRequests`, `Refusal` or `Cancelled`
    detail
        What `Connection#prompt` returns, once the agent has finished everything it did in the
        turn. `EndTurn` means the agent finished; `MaxTokens` and `MaxTurnRequests` that it ran
        out of budget, in tokens or in model requests; `Refusal` that it declined to go on;
        and `Cancelled` that the client cancelled the turn with `Connection#cancel`, which the
        agent is obliged to answer this way. On the wire the cases are `end_turn`,
        `max_tokens`, `max_turn_requests`, `refusal` and `cancelled`.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        Acp.connection.prompt(sessionId, t"Run the tests") match
          case Acp.StopReason.EndTurn   => Out.println(t"done")
          case Acp.StopReason.Cancelled => Out.println(t"cancelled")
          case other                    => Out.println(t"stopped: $other")
        ```
    entry .encodable   encodes a stop reason as its wire string
    entry .decodable   decodes a stop reason from its wire string, raising `Json.Error` for any other
        Needs a `Tactic[Json.Error]`, which an unknown string raises through with the reason
        `OutOfRange`.

  entry .PromptResult   the agent's answer to `session/prompt`: why the turn ended
    detail
        `Connection#prompt` returns only its `#stopReason`.
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed result throws `Json.Error`
    entry #stopReason   why the turn ended
    entry #_meta   the agent's implementation-defined metadata, as raw JSON

  entry .ToolKind   the kind of a tool call: read, edit, delete, move, search, execute, think, fetch or other
    detail
        Reported in `ToolCall#kind`, so that a client can show a tool call with a fitting icon
        or decide how cautious to be about permitting it. The cases are `Read`, `Edit`,
        `Delete`, `Move`, `Search`, `Execute`, `Think`, `Fetch` and `Other`; on the wire each
        is its lower-case name.
    entry .encodable   encodes a kind as its wire string
    entry .decodable   decodes a kind from its wire string; an unknown kind is `Other`
        Unlike the other enums' decoders, it never fails: the protocol lets agents add kinds,
        so one this library does not know reads as `ToolKind.Other`.

  entry .ToolCallStatus   where a tool call has got to: `Pending`, `InProgress`, `Completed`, `Failed` or `Cancelled`
    detail
        Reported in `ToolCall#status` and updated by `ToolCallUpdate#status`. On the wire the
        cases are `pending`, `in_progress`, `completed`, `failed` and `cancelled`.
    entry .encodable   encodes a status as its wire string
    entry .decodable   decodes a status from its wire string, raising `Json.Error` for any other

  entry .ToolCallLocation   a file, and optionally a line, that a tool call is working on
    detail
        Listed in `ToolCall#locations`, so that a client can follow the agent through the
        files it reads and edits.
    entry #path   the file's absolute path
    entry #line   the line within it, if the agent says

  entry .ToolCallContent   what a tool call produced: content, a file diff, or a terminal
    detail
        A sealed hierarchy of three cases, discriminated on the wire by a `type` member:
        `ToolContent` (`content`) wraps an ordinary `ContentBlock`; `ToolDiff` (`diff`) is a
        structured edit to one file; `ToolTerminal` (`terminal`) refers to a terminal the
        client created for the agent, whose output the client can show.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        def describe(content: Acp.ToolCallContent): Text = content match
          case Acp.ToolContent(Acp.TextContent(text, _)) => text
          case Acp.ToolContent(_)                        => t"(content)"
          case Acp.ToolDiff(path, _, _)                  => t"edited $path"
          case Acp.ToolTerminal(terminalId)              => t"terminal $terminalId"
        ```
    entry .encodable   encodes any of the three cases with its `type` discriminator
    entry .decodable   decodes by the `type` member, raising `Json.Error` for an unknown type

  entry .ToolContent   tool output as an ordinary content block
    detail
        A `ToolCallContent` case, `type` `content` on the wire.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; malformed content throws `Json.Error`
    entry #content   the content block

  entry .ToolDiff   tool output as a structured edit to one file: its old text and its new
    detail
        A `ToolCallContent` case, `type` `diff` on the wire. `#oldText` is `Unset` for a file
        the tool created.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed diff throws `Json.Error`
    entry #path   the file's absolute path
    entry #oldText   the file's text before the edit, or `Unset` for a new file
    entry #newText   the file's text after the edit

  entry .ToolTerminal   tool output as a reference to a terminal the client created
    detail
        A `ToolCallContent` case, `type` `terminal` on the wire. The id is one `Terminals#create`
        returned, so the client can show that terminal's output alongside the tool call.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder
    entry #terminalId   the terminal's id

  entry .ToolCall   a tool call as the agent first reports it: the `tool_call` session update
    detail
        A `SessionUpdate` case. Only the id and the title are required; everything else may be
        filled in later by a `ToolCallUpdate` with the same `#toolCallId`, so a client which
        displays tool calls keeps them by id and merges each update in.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        def register(using Acp.Registry^): Unit = Acp.updated:
          Acp.update match
            case call: Acp.ToolCall =>
              Out.println(t"${call.title} (${call.kind.let(_.show).or(t"unknown")})")

            case update: Acp.ToolCallUpdate =>
              Out.println(t"${update.toolCallId}: ${update.status.let(_.show).or(t"unchanged")}")

            case _ => ()
        ```
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed tool call throws `Json.Error`
    entry #toolCallId   the id later updates and permission requests refer to it by
    entry #title   a human-readable description of what the tool is doing
    entry #kind   the kind of tool, if the agent says
    entry #status   where the call has got to, if the agent says
    entry #content   what the call has produced so far
    entry #locations   the files the call is working on
    entry #rawInput   the tool's input, as raw JSON
    entry #rawOutput   the tool's output, as raw JSON

  entry .ToolCallUpdate   a later report about a tool call, or the tool call a permission request concerns
    detail
        A `SessionUpdate` case, `tool_call_update` on the wire. Every member but
        `#toolCallId` is optional, and a member which is present replaces what was reported
        before, so `#content` is the whole content, not an addition to it. The same shape
        carries the tool call a permission request is about, lent to a permission handler as
        `Acp.toolCall`.
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed update throws `Json.Error`
    entry #toolCallId   the id of the tool call being updated
    entry #title   a new title, or `Unset` to keep the old
    entry #kind   a new kind, or `Unset` to keep the old
    entry #status   a new status, or `Unset` to keep the old
    entry #content   the call's whole content, replacing what was reported, or `Unset` to keep it
    entry #locations   the call's whole list of locations, or `Unset` to keep it
    entry #rawInput   the tool's input, as raw JSON, or `Unset` to keep it
    entry #rawOutput   the tool's output, as raw JSON, or `Unset` to keep it

  entry .PlanPriority   how important a step of the agent's plan is: `High`, `Medium` or `Low`
    entry .encodable   encodes a priority as its wire string
    entry .decodable   decodes a priority from its wire string, raising `Json.Error` for any other

  entry .PlanStatus   where a step of the agent's plan has got to: `Pending`, `InProgress` or `Completed`
    entry .encodable   encodes a status as its wire string
    entry .decodable   decodes a status from its wire string, raising `Json.Error` for any other

  entry .PlanEntry   one step of the agent's plan: what it is, how important, and how far along
    entry #content   a human-readable description of the step
    entry #priority   how important the step is
    entry #status   where the step has got to

  entry .AvailableCommand   a slash command the agent offers in a session, with its description
    detail
        Listed in an `AvailableCommandsUpdate`, so that a client can offer the commands for
        completion as the user types a prompt.
    entry #name   the command's name, without its slash
    entry #description   what the command does
    entry #input   the command's input specification, as raw JSON, if it takes input

  entry .SessionUpdate   one `session/update` notification: something the agent reports about a session as it works
    detail
        A sealed hierarchy of the eight things an agent streams during a turn — and, during
        `Connection#loadSession`, replays from a resumed one — discriminated on the wire by
        the `sessionUpdate` member:

        - `UserMessageChunk` (`user_message_chunk`) — a piece of the user's own message, in a
          replayed session;
        - `AgentMessageChunk` (`agent_message_chunk`) — a piece of the agent's answer;
        - `AgentThoughtChunk` (`agent_thought_chunk`) — a piece of its reasoning;
        - `ToolCall` (`tool_call`) and `ToolCallUpdate` (`tool_call_update`) — a tool call
          reported, and a later change to it;
        - `Plan` (`plan`) — the agent's plan, in full;
        - `AvailableCommandsUpdate` (`available_commands_update`) — the slash commands now
          offered;
        - `CurrentModeUpdate` (`current_mode_update`) — the session's mode has changed.

        Updates reach the handler registered with `Acp.updated` in the order the agent sent
        them: they are dispatched on the connection's reader task itself, not on spawned
        tasks, since the order of chunks matters. A handler should therefore return promptly,
        and must not block on a request of the same connection.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        def register(using Acp.Registry^): Unit = Acp.updated:
          Acp.update match
            case Acp.AgentMessageChunk(Acp.TextContent(text, _)) => Out.print(text)
            case Acp.AgentThoughtChunk(Acp.TextContent(text, _)) => Out.print(t"(thinking) $text")
            case Acp.Plan(entries)                               => entries.each { entry => Out.println(entry.content) }
            case Acp.CurrentModeUpdate(modeId)                   => Out.println(t"mode: $modeId")
            case _                                               => ()
        ```
    refer espionage.Acp.updated
    refer espionage.Acp.update
    entry .encodable   encodes any update with its `sessionUpdate` discriminator
    entry .decodable   decodes by the `sessionUpdate` member, raising `Json.Error` for an unknown kind

  entry .UserMessageChunk   a piece of the user's message, as an agent replays a resumed session
    detail
        A `SessionUpdate` case, `user_message_chunk` on the wire.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder
    entry #content   the piece of content

  entry .AgentMessageChunk   a piece of the agent's answer, streamed as it is written
    detail
        A `SessionUpdate` case, `agent_message_chunk` on the wire. A turn's answer arrives as
        many chunks, in order; printing each `TextContent` as it arrives shows the answer as
        the agent writes it.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder
    entry #content   the piece of content, usually a `TextContent`

  entry .AgentThoughtChunk   a piece of the agent's reasoning, streamed as it thinks
    detail
        A `SessionUpdate` case, `agent_thought_chunk` on the wire: text a client may show
        apart from the answer, or not at all.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder
    entry #content   the piece of content

  entry .Plan   the agent's plan for the turn, in full
    detail
        A `SessionUpdate` case, `plan` on the wire. Each report is the whole plan, replacing
        the last, not a change to it.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder
    entry #entries   the plan's steps, in order

  entry .AvailableCommandsUpdate   the slash commands the agent now offers in the session
    detail
        A `SessionUpdate` case, `available_commands_update` on the wire. Each report is the
        whole list.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder
    entry #availableCommands   the commands now offered

  entry .CurrentModeUpdate   the session's mode has changed
    detail
        A `SessionUpdate` case, `current_mode_update` on the wire, sent when the mode changes
        at the agent's initiative or in answer to `Connection#setMode`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder
    entry #currentModeId   the id of the mode the session is now in

  entry .PermissionOptionKind   what choosing a permission option means: allow or reject, once or always
    detail
        The cases are `AllowOnce`, `AllowAlways`, `RejectOnce` and `RejectAlways` (on the wire
        `allow_once`, `allow_always`, `reject_once` and `reject_always`). The kinds let a
        client pick an option by policy rather than by the agent's wording:

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        def allowOnce(using Acp.Registry^): Unit = Acp.permission:
          Acp.options.filter(_.kind == Acp.PermissionOptionKind.AllowOnce).head
          . lay(Acp.Cancelled): option => Acp.Selected(option.optionId)
        ```
    entry .encodable   encodes a kind as its wire string
    entry .decodable   decodes a kind from its wire string, raising `Json.Error` for any other

  entry .PermissionOption   one answer the agent offers to a permission request
    detail
        A permission handler is lent the offered options as `Acp.options`, and answers with
        `Acp.Selected(option.optionId)` for the one it chooses.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder
    entry #optionId   the id to answer with, in `Selected`
    entry #name   the option's label, to show the user
    entry #kind   what choosing the option means

  entry .RequestPermissionOutcome   the answer to a permission request: `Selected(optionId)` or `Cancelled`
    detail
        A permission handler's result. `Selected` names one of the options offered;
        `Cancelled` declines to choose. `Cancelled` is also the answer the protocol requires to
        every permission request pending when its turn is cancelled, and the connection
        supplies it itself: a request which arrives after `Connection#cancel` is answered
        `Cancelled` without calling the handler, and one already in the handler when the
        cancellation arrives is answered `Cancelled` when the handler returns, its selection
        discarded. On the wire the outcome is discriminated by an `outcome` member.
    entry .encodable   encodes an outcome with its `outcome` discriminator
    entry .decodable   decodes by the `outcome` member, raising `Json.Error` for an unknown outcome

  entry .Selected   the permission answer which chooses one of the offered options
    entry #optionId   the chosen option's id, from `PermissionOption#optionId`

  entry .Cancelled   the permission answer which chooses nothing, required for a cancelled turn
      A `RequestPermissionOutcome`. A handler returns it to decline, and it is the answer to
      a request for which no handler was registered. It is distinct from
      `StopReason.Cancelled`, which is how a cancelled turn ends.

  entry .RequestPermissionResult   the client's response to `session/request_permission`, wrapping the outcome
    entry #outcome   the handler's answer, or `Cancelled`

  entry .ReadTextFileResult   the client's response to `fs/read_text_file`: the file's content
    entry #content   the text read

  entry .CreateTerminalResult   the client's response to `terminal/create`: the new terminal's id
    entry #terminalId   the id `Terminals#create` returned

  entry .TerminalExitStatus   how a terminal's command ended: its exit code, or the signal which killed it
    entry #exitCode   the command's exit code, if it exited
    entry #signal   the name of the signal which killed it, if one did

  entry .TerminalOutputResult   a terminal's output so far, and its exit status if it has finished
    entry #output   the output captured so far
    entry #truncated   whether output was dropped from the start to stay within the byte limit
    entry #exitStatus   how the command ended, or `Unset` while it runs

  entry .Terminals   the five terminal operations a client offers an agent, supplied together
    detail
        The protocol advertises terminal support as a single capability, so the operations are
        all or nothing: a client implements this trait and registers it with `Acp.terminals`,
        and `ClientCapabilities#terminal` is then `true`. Each method is called on a task of
        its own, with the session id it concerns; the terminal ids are the client's to mint.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        def register(using Acp.Registry^): Unit = Acp.terminals:
          new Acp.Terminals:
            def create
              ( sessionId:       Text,
                command:         Text,
                args:            List[Text],
                env:             List[Acp.EnvVariable],
                cwd:             Optional[Text],
                outputByteLimit: Optional[Long] )
            :   Text =
              t"term1"

            def output(sessionId: Text, terminalId: Text): Acp.TerminalOutputResult =
              Acp.TerminalOutputResult(t"", false)

            def waitForExit(sessionId: Text, terminalId: Text): Acp.TerminalExitStatus =
              Acp.TerminalExitStatus(0)

            def kill(sessionId: Text, terminalId: Text): Unit = ()
            def release(sessionId: Text, terminalId: Text): Unit = ()
        ```

        A request for a terminal operation when no bundle was registered is answered with a
        `MethodNotFound` error, so a misbehaving agent is told rather than left waiting.
    entry #create   starts a command in a new terminal, returning the terminal's id
        Called with the command, its arguments and environment, an optional working directory,
        and an optional limit in bytes on the output to retain; the oldest output beyond it
        is dropped, and `TerminalOutputResult#truncated` reports that.
    entry #output   the terminal's output so far, and its exit status if it has finished
    entry #waitForExit   blocks until the terminal's command exits, returning how it ended
    entry #kill   kills the terminal's command, keeping the terminal for its output
    entry #release   kills the command if it still runs, and frees the terminal

  entry .Turned1   a handler's context: the session id, one tagged payload, and an error emitter
      `(sessionId: Text aka "sessionId", payload: payload, emit: Emit[Acp.Error]^) ?=> result`.
      Each payload is tagged with `aka`, so that the contextual accessors (`Acp.update`, and
      so on) find it by name. Everything lent is a context parameter and the result is pure,
      so nothing lent to a handler can leave it. `UpdateHandler` is one.
  entry .Turned2   a handler's context: the session id, two tagged payloads, and an error emitter
      As `Turned1`, with two payloads. `PermissionHandler` and `WriteFileHandler` are ones.
  entry .Turned3   a handler's context: the session id, three tagged payloads, and an error emitter
      As `Turned1`, with three payloads. `ReadFileHandler` is one.

  entry .UpdateHandler   the handler `Acp.updated` takes: sees each `SessionUpdate`, returns nothing
      Lent `Acp.sessionId` and `Acp.update`. It runs on the connection's reader task, in the
      order the agent sent the updates.
  entry .PermissionHandler   the handler `Acp.permission` takes: chooses among the offered options
      Lent `Acp.sessionId`, `Acp.toolCall` (the `ToolCallUpdate` the request concerns) and
      `Acp.options`, and returns a `RequestPermissionOutcome`. It runs on a task of its own,
      so it may block — waiting for the user, for minutes — without stalling the turn's
      updates.
  entry .ReadFileHandler   the handler `Acp.readFile` takes: returns a file's text
      Lent `Acp.sessionId`, `Acp.path`, and the optional `Acp.line` (one-based) to start at and
      `Acp.limit` on the number of lines, and returns the text. It may serve the client's own
      view of the file, such as an editor's unsaved buffer.
  entry .WriteFileHandler   the handler `Acp.writeFile` takes: writes a file's text
      Lent `Acp.sessionId`, `Acp.path` and `Acp.content`, and returns nothing.

  entry .connection   the live connection `Acp.connect` lends to its second block
      `Acp.connection` reads the `Acp.Connection` context parameter, with its singleton type,
      so `Acp.connection.prompt(…)` is a call on the very connection in scope. It is
      available only inside `connect`'s second block, or a method which takes a `using
      Acp.Connection`.
  entry .sessionId   in a handler, the id of the session the request or update concerns
      Every handler is lent it.
  entry .update   in an update handler, the `SessionUpdate` being reported
  entry .toolCall   in a permission handler, the tool call the agent wants permission for
      A `ToolCallUpdate`: its id, and whatever the agent says of the call it is about to make.
  entry .options   in a permission handler, the answers the agent offers, as `PermissionOption`s
  entry .path   in a file handler, the absolute path of the file to read or write
  entry .line   in a read handler, the one-based line to start reading at, or `Unset` for the start
  entry .limit   in a read handler, the most lines to return, or `Unset` for no limit
  entry .content   in a write handler, the text to write
      Not to be confused with the `#content` members of the content-carrying records: this is
      the accessor, and needs a write handler's context.

  entry .updated   registers the handler for `session/update`: sees every update of every session
      An `UpdateHandler`, lent `Acp.sessionId` and `Acp.update`. Registering none discards the
      updates. Needs an `Acp.Registry` in scope, as in the first block of `Acp.connect`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      def register(using Acp.Registry^): Unit = Acp.updated:
        Acp.update match
          case Acp.AgentMessageChunk(Acp.TextContent(text, _)) => Out.print(text)
          case _                                               => ()
      ```
  entry .permission   registers the handler for `session/request_permission`
      A `PermissionHandler`, lent `Acp.sessionId`, `Acp.toolCall` and `Acp.options`, and
      returning `Acp.Selected(optionId)` or `Acp.Cancelled`. With no handler registered, every
      request is answered `Cancelled`. A request in a cancelled turn is answered `Cancelled`
      whatever the handler says.
  entry .readFile   registers the handler for `fs/read_text_file`, and advertises the capability
      A `ReadFileHandler`, lent `Acp.path`, `Acp.line` and `Acp.limit`, returning the text.
      Registering it sets `FsCapabilities#readTextFile`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem

      def files(using Acp.Registry^): Unit = Acp.readFile:
        Acp.path.as[Path on Linux].open[File]()(file.read[Text])
      ```
  entry .writeFile   registers the handler for `fs/write_text_file`, and advertises the capability
      A `WriteFileHandler`, lent `Acp.path` and `Acp.content`. Registering it sets
      `FsCapabilities#writeTextFile`.
  entry .terminals   registers the `Terminals` bundle, and advertises the terminal capability
      The five terminal operations are registered together, since the protocol advertises
      them as one capability. Registering them sets `ClientCapabilities#terminal`.
  entry .capabilities   adjusts the client capabilities derived from the registrations, last, at initialization
      An escape hatch: `Acp.capabilities(adjust)` is applied to the derived
      `ClientCapabilities` just before `initialize` is sent, so a client may withdraw a
      capability, or declare one it serves by other means. The derived declaration is right
      for almost every client.

  entry .connect   connects to an agent for the duration of a block: registers handlers, initializes, lends a connection
    detail
        `Acp.connect(agent, observer)(register)(lambda)`. The first block registers the
        client's handlers on the lent `Acp.Registry`; when it returns, the registry is consumed
        and the client's capabilities are derived from it, so nothing can be registered once
        the exchange begins. Then the exchange opens — a writer task drains outgoing messages
        onto the agent's input, and a reader task routes what comes back — and `initialize` is
        exchanged, raising `Acp.Error` with reason `Incompatible` if the agent speaks a
        protocol version other than `Acp.version`. The second block then runs with a live,
        initialized `Acp.Connection`, reached as `Acp.connection`, and its value is the value
        of `connect`. When it returns, the exchange is torn down and a subprocess agent is
        killed, so nothing outlives the agent it speaks to.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
        import probates.awaitProbate
        import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.virtualThreads
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

        val reason: Acp.StopReason = supervise:
          Acp.connect(Acp.Agent(sh"claude-code-acp")):
            Acp.updated:
              Acp.update match
                case Acp.AgentMessageChunk(Acp.TextContent(text, _)) => Out.print(text)
                case _                                               => ()

          . apply:
              val session = Acp.connection.newSession(t"/workspace")
              Acp.connection.prompt(session.sessionId, t"Explain this repository")
        ```

        It needs a `Monitor` (so it sits inside `supervise`), a `Probate`, `Diagnostics`, a
        `WorkingDirectory` and an `Environment` (for launching a subprocess agent), and a
        `Tactic[Acp.Error]`. A subprocess which cannot be launched raises `Acp.Error` with
        reason `Internal` and the launch failure's message. The connection block may capture
        the monitor freely — a block which prompts on one task and cancels from another, say
        — since a monitor is a shared capability.

        Messages from the agent are routed by kind. Responses are correlated with the requests
        awaiting them, so several requests may be in flight and answered in any order.
        `session/update` notifications are dispatched on the reader task, in order. Requests —
        permissions, file operations, terminals — are each dispatched on a spawned task, so a
        permission handler waiting on the user stalls neither the updates nor the responses.
        A request for a method this library does not model is answered `Method not found`, and
        an unmodelled notification is ignored.

        `observer`, if given, sees every message in both directions as it crosses the
        transport; the default is `Acp.Observer.Silent`.
    refer espionage.Acp.Agent
    refer espionage.Acp.Connection
    refer espionage.Acp.Registry
    refer espionage.Acp.Observer

  entry .Observer   a hook on the raw traffic: every message as it crosses the transport
    detail
        An agent's standard output is the wire, and a client may want a log or a protocol
        trace of what it exchanges: an observer sees each message as its JSON text — inbound
        before parsing, so a message which fails to decode is observed too, and outbound
        before framing, so both directions read alike. It is passed to `Acp.connect` as
        `observer`.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        val trace = new Acp.Observer:
          def received(message: Text): Unit = Err.println(t"← $message")
          def sent(message: Text): Unit = Err.println(t"→ $message")
        ```

        The writer task calls `#sent` and the reader task `#received`, at once, so an
        implementation must be safe to call from two threads. It is a shared, durable
        capability.
    entry .Silent   the observer which does nothing, as a pure value; `connect`'s default
    entry .silent   the given which supplies `Silent` contextually; in implicit scope
    entry #received   called with each message read from the agent, before it is parsed
    entry #sent   called with each message sent to the agent, as its encoded body, before it is framed

  entry .Agent   an agent this process can speak to: a command to launch, or a pair of streams
    detail
        A plain value saying where the agent is, holding no channel of its own: the channel is
        opened when `Acp.connect` begins and closed when it ends. `Agent.Process(command)` is
        an agent launched as a subprocess, whose standard input and output carry the protocol,
        and `Agent.Streams(input, output)` one already running behind a pair of `java.io`
        streams — in this process, behind a socket, or a fixture in a test. `Agent(command)`
        and `Agent.streams(input, output)` build each, widened to `Agent`.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        val launched: Acp.Agent = Acp.Agent(sh"claude-code-acp")
        val gemini: Acp.Agent = Acp.Agent(sh"gemini --experimental-acp")
        ```

        A subprocess agent is launched with a silent exec log, since its standard output is
        the wire, and is killed, not waited for, when the connection ends.
    refer espionage.Acp.connect
    entry .apply   an agent launched as a subprocess: `Acp.Agent(command)`
        Takes a guillotine `Command`, written with `sh"…"`.
    entry .streams   an agent already running behind an input and an output stream
        Which stream is which is from this process's point of view: `input` is what the agent
        writes, `output` what it reads. Each message the agent writes is delivered as soon as
        its terminating newline arrives, so a fixture writing to a pipe need only flush after
        each line.

  entry .Error   a fault in an ACP exchange, or one the agent reported, by its JSON-RPC reason
    detail
        Error 148: _the ACP operation failed because `reason`_, numbered by reason (148.1 for
        `Parse` through 148.7 for `Incompatible`). Each `Reason` carries the JSON-RPC wire
        code the protocol assigns it: `Parse` −32700, `InvalidRequest` −32600,
        `MethodNotFound` −32601, `InvalidParams` −32602, `Internal` −32603 and `AuthRequired`
        −32000; `Incompatible` is this library's own, raised by `Acp.connect` when the agent
        answers `initialize` with another protocol version.

        The connection's requests raise one, through the `Tactic[Acp.Error]` in scope, for an
        error response from the agent, with the reason recovered from the wire code by
        `.reason` (a code outside the standard set is `Internal`) and the agent's message as
        `#details`. An agent which needs authentication answers with `AuthRequired`; the client
        then calls `Connection#authenticate`.

        A handler raises one through the `Emit[Acp.Error]` it is lent, and the client answers
        the agent's request with an error response carrying the reason's code and `#response`
        as the message:

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        def register(using Acp.Registry^): Unit = Acp.readFile:
          if Acp.path.starts(t"/secret") then
            raise(Acp.Error(Acp.Error.Reason.InvalidParams, t"not readable"))
          t""
        ```

        Constructing one needs `Diagnostics` in scope, as every fulminate error does.
    entry .Reason   why the operation failed, each with its JSON-RPC wire `code` and its `number`
      detail
          `Parse` (1, −32700): _the message could not be parsed as JSON_; `InvalidRequest` (2,
          −32600): _the message was not a valid JSON-RPC request_; `MethodNotFound` (3,
          −32601): _the method name was not recognised_; `InvalidParams` (4, −32602): _the
          parameters were not valid for the requested method_; `Internal` (5, −32603): _an
          internal error occurred_; `AuthRequired` (6, −32000): _the agent requires
          authentication_; `Incompatible` (7, no wire code): _the agent does not support the
          protocol version this client implements_.
      entry #number   the sub-number of error 148
      entry #code   the JSON-RPC wire code, or `0` for `Incompatible`, which has none
    entry .reason   the reason a wire code names, or `Unset` for a code outside the standard set
        The inverse of `Reason#code`, for reading an error response. `0` is never a wire code,
        so it never yields `Incompatible`.
    entry .communicable   renders a reason as its message, _the agent requires authentication_ and the like
    entry #reason   why the operation failed
    entry #details   the agent's message, or the message given when the error was raised, if any
    entry #response   the message sent in an error response: the details, or the reason's description

  entry .Registry   where a client's handlers are recorded, lent to the first block of `connect`
    detail
        The target of the registration combinators: `Acp.connect` makes one, lends it to the
        registration block as a context parameter, and consumes it when the block returns, so
        registration after the exchange begins is impossible by construction. Its constructor
        is private, and it is a capability which cannot escape the block. A method of one's
        own which registers handlers takes it as `using Acp.Registry^`.

        Its slots — one public `var` per combinator, named for it with a trailing `0` — are
        public only because the inline combinators assign them, and each handler slot is
        typed `AnyRef | Null`: a union mentioning a context-function type would freshen its
        capture sets at every adaptation, so the typed boundary is the combinator's parameter,
        where a handler closing over the registry is rejected. Register through the
        combinators, not the slots.
    refer espionage.Acp.connect
    refer espionage.Acp.ClientCapabilities
    entry .Slot   the box a registered handler is kept in
      detail
          A context-function value adapts to any expected type which is not a context
          function by being applied, so a handler cannot inhabit an `Optional` or `AnyRef`
          slot directly: `Slot[handler](value)` holds it intact, and the service unboxes it
          when it dispatches.
      entry #value   the handler
    entry #updated0   the `updated` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #permission0   the `permission` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #readFile0   the `readFile` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #writeFile0   the `writeFile` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #terminal0   the `terminals` bundle, boxed, or `null`
    entry #adjust0   the `capabilities` adjustment, or `Unset`

  entry .Connection   the client's handle on a running agent, lent by `connect` for the duration of a block
    detail
        What `Acp.connect` lends its second block, reached as `Acp.connection`. It is a
        capability, disposed of when the block returns, so it cannot outlive the agent it
        speaks to. Its constructor is private.

        A request blocks its caller until the agent's response arrives, but never blocks the
        reader, so several may be in flight at once, on different tasks, and be answered out
        of order: one task may hold a prompt turn open while another cancels it.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        val session = Acp.connection.newSession(t"/workspace")
        val turn = async(Acp.connection.prompt(session.sessionId, t"Refactor the parser"))
        Acp.connection.cancel(session.sessionId)
        turn.await()   // Acp.StopReason.Cancelled
        ```

        Every request raises `Acp.Error`, through the `Tactic[Acp.Error]` in scope, for an
        error response. The connection is obligatory's `JsonRpc` underneath, with `#Origin`
        the agent's interface `AcpAgent`, and `#send` sends a message of a method this library
        does not model.
    refer espionage.Acp.connect
    refer espionage.AcpAgent
    entry #Origin   the far end of the connection: `AcpAgent`
    entry #lifecycle   the generated client for `AcpAgentLifecycle`: `initialize` and `authenticate`
        Calling it directly bypasses the error conversion and version check the connection's
        own methods make.
    entry #sessions   the generated client for `AcpAgentSession`: the `session/*` methods
        As `#lifecycle`, prefer the connection's own methods.
    entry #send   sends a message exactly as given, minting no id and awaiting no answer
        The raw seam for an extension method or a notification this library does not model.
        A request sent this way has its response routed nowhere, so it is for notifications.
    entry #initialize   the handshake: sends the protocol version and the client's capabilities
        `Acp.connect` calls it before lending the connection, so a client does not. It raises
        `Acp.Error` with reason `Incompatible` if the agent answers with a protocol version
        other than `Acp.version`, and records the agent's answer for `#agentCapabilities` and
        `#authMethods`.
    entry #authenticate   authenticates with one of the methods the agent offered, by its id
        For an agent which answered a request with `Acp.Error.Reason.AuthRequired`: the id is
        an `AuthMethod#id` from `#authMethods`.
    entry #newSession   opens a session in a working directory, with optional MCP servers
        `newSession(cwd, mcpServers)` returns a `NewSessionResult`, whose `sessionId` every
        later call about the session takes. `cwd` should be an absolute path: the agent works
        relative to it.
    entry #loadSession   resumes a session by its id, replaying its history through the update handler
        The agent replays the whole conversation as `session/update` notifications — routed to
        the handler registered with `Acp.updated`, `UserMessageChunk`s included — before this
        returns. Only an agent whose `AgentCapabilities#loadSession` is `true` supports it.
    entry #prompt   one prompt turn: blocks until the agent reports why the turn ended
        `prompt(sessionId, content)` takes a `List[ContentBlock]`, and `prompt(sessionId,
        text)` a single `TextContent`. It returns the `StopReason` once the agent has finished
        everything it did in the turn, while the turn's updates stream concurrently to the
        update handler. Opening a turn clears the session's cancellation, so a client may
        prompt again after cancelling.
    entry #cancel   cancels the session's current turn
        A notification, not a request: the turn's outcome is the in-flight `#prompt`, which the
        agent must complete with `StopReason.Cancelled`. Permission requests pending when this
        is called, and any which arrive before the next turn opens, are answered `Cancelled`,
        as the protocol requires.
    entry #setMode   switches the session to one of its modes, by id
        The ids are the `SessionMode#id`s in `NewSessionResult#modes`.
    entry #agentCapabilities   what the agent reported it can do, at initialization
    entry #authMethods   the ways the agent offered to authenticate, at initialization

entry .AcpAgentLifecycle   the wire trait for `initialize` and `authenticate`, the client→agent handshake
  detail
      One of the `@rpc` traits which, together, make up the agent's interface `AcpAgent`;
      split out so that obligatory's `JsonRpc.proxy` generates a class of manageable size per
      trait. `Acp.Connection#lifecycle` is the generated client for it. A program needs it
      only to build a connection of its own; `Acp.Connection` wraps it.
  refer espionage.AcpAgent
  entry #initialize   sends `initialize` with the protocol version and client capabilities
  entry #authenticate   sends `authenticate` with an auth method's id

entry .AcpAgentSession   the wire trait for the `session/*` methods the client calls on the agent
    `session/new`, `session/load`, `session/prompt`, `session/cancel` (a notification, so
    returning `Unit`) and `session/set_mode`, each a method of that name, taking the
    protocol's own parameter names and types. `Acp.Connection#sessions` is the generated
    client for it, and `Acp.Connection`'s `newSession`, `loadSession`, `prompt`, `cancel` and
    `setMode` wrap it.

entry .AcpAgent   the agent's whole interface, as the client calls it: `AcpAgentLifecycle` and `AcpAgentSession`
    The union of the client→agent wire traits, and the `JsonRpc` `Origin` of an
    `Acp.Connection`. Nothing implements it on the client side: it is the type of the far
    end.

entry .AcpClientSession   the wire trait for what the agent calls on the client about a session
    `session/update`, the protocol's only notification in this direction, and
    `session/request_permission`, a request the agent blocks on. Implemented by the service
    `Acp.connect` builds, which routes them to the handlers registered with `Acp.updated` and
    `Acp.permission`.

entry .AcpClientFs   the wire trait for the agent's filesystem requests: `fs/read_text_file` and `fs/write_text_file`
    Implemented by the service `Acp.connect` builds, which routes them to the handlers
    registered with `Acp.readFile` and `Acp.writeFile`, and answers `MethodNotFound` for one
    which was not registered.

entry .AcpClientTerminal   the wire trait for the agent's terminal requests: `terminal/create`, `output`, `wait_for_exit`, `kill`, `release`
    Implemented by the service `Acp.connect` builds, which routes them to the `Acp.Terminals`
    bundle registered with `Acp.terminals`, and answers `MethodNotFound` if none was.

entry .AcpClient   the client's whole interface, as the agent calls it: session, filesystem and terminal methods
    The union of `AcpClientSession`, `AcpClientFs` and `AcpClientTerminal`. The service
    `Acp.connect` builds implements it, with one generated JSON-RPC dispatcher per part, so
    a program implements it itself only to serve the protocol by other means than
    `Acp.connect`.
